# Clean Code Cheatsheet

Summary of **Clean Code** by *Robert Cecil Martin*, plus additional guidelines for cleaner code.

Source: https://github.com/muazsh/Clean-Code-Cheatsheet/blob/main/README.md

## General Rules

- If you want to go fast, get done quickly, and make code easy to write, make it easy to read.
- **The Boy Scout Rule:** Leave the campground cleaner than you found it.
- **Meaningful names:**
  - Use intention-revealing names: the name of a variable, function, or class should answer the big questions.
  - Classes and objects should have noun or noun phrase names.
  - Methods should have verb or verb phrase names.
- Follow one naming convention.
- Follow a unified format for indentations, whitespaces, parentheses, new lines, and grouping. Prefer a tool for this (e.g. Clang Format, Visual Studio Format).
- Prefer polymorphism to if/else or switch/case; it reduces branching in the code.
- Prefer exceptions to returning error codes; they require immediate handling and reduce if-statement noise.
- Extract try/catch blocks into functions; they introduce noise in the main context.
- Continuously refine and refactor your code. Keep it as clean and simple as it can be. Never let the rot get started.
- Follow Kent Beck's four rules of Simple Design:
  - **Run all the tests:** a comprehensively tested system is verifiable and deployable; this pushes toward small classes, SRP, and DI.
  - **No duplication:** a primary enemy of a well-designed system.
  - **Expresses the intent of the programmer:** small classes/functions, good names, follow standards, tests.
  - **Minimizes the number of classes and methods:** not all classes need interfaces; not all object classes must be split into data classes and behavior classes.

## Functions

- Functions should do one thing. They should do it well. They should do it only.
- Arguments: the fewer the better (and at the same abstraction level as the function).
- Do not return or pass null.

## Classes

- Hide internal state and expose methods.
- **Data vs objects:** in complex systems, sometimes you add new data types (objects/OO fit best); sometimes you add new functions (procedural code and data structures fit best).
- **Law of Demeter:** minimize coupling.
- **Separation of concerns:** design patterns and dependency injection help apply this principle.

## Comments

- Comments must **not** explain complexity; the code should explain itself.
- Good comments describe technical aspects, returned values, TODOs, or importance of non-obvious code.
- Do not comment out code; delete it.

## Tests

- Tests are as important as the project. If you let tests rot, your code will rot too. Keep tests clean.
- Test code should be as clean as production code (readable).
- The higher the test coverage, the less fear you have.
- One test should test one concept.
- Minimize asserts in one test.
- Tests must be **Fast, Independent, Repeatable, Self-Validating, Timely** (just before the production code).

## Third-Party Packages

- Do not expose third-party interfaces in your APIs; use them only internally.
- Wrap third-party libraries rather than exposing the library itself everywhere.
- Test third-party packages.

## Concurrency

- Keep concurrency-related code separate from other code.
- Use what the language offers before building your own.
- Keep synchronized sections small.
- Think about shutdown early and get it working early.
- **Testing threaded code:**
  - Treat spurious failures as candidate threading issues.
  - Get your non-threaded code working first.
  - Make your threaded code pluggable.
  - Make your threaded code tunable.
  - Run with more threads than processors.
  - Run on different platforms.
  - Instrument your code to try and force failures.

## Implementation Principles

- **KISS (Keep It Simple, Stupid):** avoid unnecessary complexity.
- **DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself):** as above.
- **YAGNI (You Aren't Gonna Need It):** do not write code because you think you will need it.

## Design: SOLID Principles

- **Single Responsibility:** any function must have one job; any class must have one concept; modules and libraries should introduce services in one context.
- **Open-Closed Principle:** entities (classes, modules, libs) must be open for extension but closed for modification.
- **Liskov Substitution Principle:** replacing an object of a class with one of a derived class must not break program behavior.
- **Interface Segregation Principle:** multiple focused interfaces passed on demand beat one interface with functions the client never needs.
- **Dependency Injection:** reduces coupling; pass needed services in instead of creating objects internally.

## More

- Use design patterns when applicable.
- Avoid anti-patterns (e.g. God classes).
- Avoid code smells: many parameters, bad names, magic numbers, etc.
- Use a static code analyzer (e.g. CppCheck, ReSharper).
